welcome to the Macmillan report I'm  Marilyn Wilkes your host and our guest  is Kamari Clarke professor of  anthropology at Yale University and  chair of the Council on African Studies  at the Macmillan Center professor  Clark's areas of research explore issues  related to religious nationalism legal  institutions international law the  interface between culture and power and  its relationship to the modernity of  race and late capitalist globalization  her recent articles and books have  focused on religious and legal movements  and the related production of knowledge  and power including the 2004 publication  of mapping Yoruba networks power and  agency in the making of transnational  communities today we'll talk with  Professor Clark about her newest book  fictions of Justice the International  Criminal Court and the challenge of  legal pluralism in sub-saharan Africa  welcome professor Clark thank you thank  you very much please tell us about the  premise of your book well mapping yerba  networks was my earlier book and in that  book I attempted to think through how  one would do an anthropology that goes  beyond spatial configurations in this  book fictions of Justice what I attempt  to do is is take on some of the same  conceptual trajectories but instead map  them through legal frameworks and thus  the title fictions of justice it's an  attempt to think about that which  constitutes justice to ask questions  about the very things that seem to be  natural and straight forward often when  we talk about justice when we say  justice has been done etc it we presume  that we all mean the same thing and the  book really is an exploration over the  controversies of justice the both  transnational and national I examine an  emergent movement the rise of the  International Criminal Court the  formation of the court as well as the  stations related to the the making of  the International Criminal Court what we  call the ICC okay what is the ICC and  what countries fall under its  jurisdiction right yes well the ICC is  is known to most as an independent  international judiciary so it has the  teeth of of a court as one would expect  it there 110 countries that are member  states that are under the jurisdiction  of the court countries from every part  of the world really the u.s. is not a  signatory but their number of countries  many many countries that are more than  half of the world's nation states are  are under the jurisdiction of the court  okay and why focus on sub-saharan Africa  why did you pick there well um part of  the world yeah it well I'm an African  estoy also an American astir force and  so in many ways analytically it made  sense for me to examine what was  happening in this region visa vie the  you know why state sign international  treaties and why this treaty in  particular but some of the  decision-making really wasn't mine the  first case is our african-based cases  they're not you know china-based cases  they're not Latin American base cases  they're african-based cases so it was  also quite straight forward that yes  indeed i followed the cases more than  followed my own research areas ok so the  term legal pluralism what is it and what  is the challenge in sub-saharan Africa  right yeah that's and that's precisely  the subtext of the book the challenge of  legal pluralism in sub-saharan Africa  well legal pluralism is somewhat of a  new concept I guess a twentieth-century  concept that emerged out of an interest  to think about dual legal systems that  coexist and a lot of legal scholars  legal anthropologists the range of  critical legal theorists etc have used  it almost as a as a way to go beyond  term known as relativism right so to go  beyond relativism but to think about the  ways that systems coexist and come  eat my argument are part of the premise  is that we have to go beyond this  conception beyond the notion of  plurality that that legal systems  coexist I the the challenge is that the  systems coexist in relations of power  they coexist alongside other  negotiations that are connected to other  powerful very powerful institutions the  World Bank the IMS IMF powerful  nationalist movements etc and to simply  see them as legal systems that exist in  and of themselves without looking at  systems of hegemony the ways that these  systems work in relation to other  spheres of power is to miss read the  coexistence of legal systems and so the  attempt is to to not only look at these  legal systems alongside each other so  the rise of Islamic sharia in northern  Nigeria alongside the emergence of ICC  power right it's to go beyond simply  looking at the dualities and instead to  think about what are some of the other  dynamics that are at play here what are  the power relations wire States signing  these treaties in the first place so so  really the attempt is to go beyond  simply this comparative frame and to  think about the power relations that  sometimes imbalances complexities that  are fundamental to the the so-called  coexistence of these legal spheres so  what methodology did you use in your  book how did you gather the data yeah  it's it's it's a good question actually  and and my methods were somewhat  unorthodox in the sense that I'm a  cultural anthropologist who is  interested in international relations  has some background in political science  and the methods that I use in their  ethnography it involves doing  ethnography but it's transnational and  multi-sided ethnography it involved I  mean in your most North most Orthodox  tway doing interviews of course doing  focus groups doing standard observations  that any social scientists would would  engage in but it also in  involved late nights after a diplomatic  herring a closed session at the bar  drinking having a few drinks to hear you  know to debrief what the the latest  controversy was at a given meeting or it  involved me going to law school for a  year to learn the fundamentals of of  criminal law and and some basic legal  jurisprudential questions it also  involved working with NGOs in various  parts of sub-saharan Africa so doing  some constitutional reform work so some  engaged what we call engaged ethnography  which goes beyond simply data collection  but also participant and participate  participation and engaged political  mobilization related work and and that  is often seen as a less Orthodox  approach but but I tell you this kind of  work this kind of transnational quite  messy work that examines an emergent  movement that has many tentacles in many  different places involves quite messy  methodology so those were those are the  highlights of the work you know  something just dawned on me when you  said you know you would go to the  courtroom and then you know afterwards  go to the bar it dawned on me where does  the International Criminal Court travel  around to different countries based on  the trials they are hearing or is it in  one location right well the court that  the building the structure you know the  thing called the International Criminal  Court is in the hague okay just it's in  the hague it's and it's in the  netherlands but before it it was in The  Hague it the the court was really an  idea in 1998 the signing of the the Rome  Statute led to the concept and from the  concept there were preparatory committee  meetings at the UN so every couple of  months or every four months or so  diplomats from around the world would  converge to work on this document that  then became the the International  Criminal Court that became the  Constitution that was fundamentally the  constitution of the court so it was an  idea that became a building but still it  lives outside of the structure and I  think  that's what is quite interesting and  phenomenal about this this thing this  movement this movement that exists  alongside the the rise of the rule of  law around the world really but yes to  you know in a straightforward way there  it is a building and it's a building  that houses many from the the defense  prosecutor to the lead prosecutor to the  judges to to the you know the audience  the people who who visit and and and  watch the cases it is it is a building  that's housed in The Hague okay let's  talk about your findings what  conclusions have you reached tell us  about some of them well there are  numbers of conclusions are two prongs in  in some ways because i'm writing to  multiple audiences in this book and it's  it's quite the challenge on one hand i'm  writing to for anthropologists I'm  engaging notions of legal pluralism I'm  thinking about transnational  methodologies how one studies up as it  were in historically anthropologists  have tended to study local people in a  way in villages etc and this has changed  tremendously over the last decade and  and so I'm in conversation with  anthropologist thinking about the  limitations of this concept called legal  pluralism thinking about the fictions of  this notion that we know as justice and  trying to shift the debate in a way to  push us to think about emergent  movements and hegemonic movements  movements that are that are  fundamentally intertwined with the whole  set of other new and emerging legal  institutions the other is I'm in  conversation with NGOs and with sort of  government and with the prosecutors and  lawyers and human rights activists were  part of the court and in part I'm asking  them and thinking through this this idea  of justice but also thinking about the  the methods of deliberation right how  how might we envision a different world  in a different constitution or a  different statute what are the problems  of the contemporary statute what kinds  of conversations  do we need to be having in order to  think through what justice might mean in  these other domains these other locales  and this is all part of a conversation  an ongoing one that I have and I'm  having with them it's not always a  popular one my position isn't  necessarily a popular one among NGO  activists etc but it's an important one  it's certainly a conversation that needs  to be had and then lastly what is the  most surprising thing you found in doing  the research for fictions of Justice and  and and writing the book yeah the most  surprising probably was how different  people's definitions of what justice is  our one person's justice is another  person's retribution one person's  justice is another person's pain and I  actually thought that I perhaps was had  a sense that there was more in common  than not and after almost eight nine  years of research in this area realized  that that this this concept is a concept  that is quite far reaching and is  fundamentally culturally constructed and  means everything and nothing to the very  same people who are being asked what  justice is so that's probably the most  surprising okay well thank you so much  for being here with us today and sharing  some of your research well thank you for  having me Marilyn for more information  about professor Clark and your work  please visit our website at yale edu  backslash macmillan report be sure to  join us again for another episode of the  macmillan report made possible through  funding from the Whitney and Betty  Macmillan Center for International and  area studies at Yale  you
 

